  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/callochromis-pleurospilus-kigoma-2/",
      "html": "<p>The species becomes only rarely over 10 cm long and can therefore still be counted well among the dwarf cichlids.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/callochromis-pleurospilus-kigoma-2/",
      "html": "<p>This sand cichlid originates from Lake Tanganyika, where it is found almost all over the lake with the exception of the extreme south.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/callochromis-pleurospilus-kigoma-2/",
      "html": "<p>It inhabits shallow shore areas with sandy bottom and sprinkled stones. There, the animals live in big swarms. When in danger, these cichlids dig in itself – like a flounder – lying on the side. Only one eye remains uncovered by the sand. So the animal observes its surroundings, until the danger is past.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/callochromis-pleurospilus-kigoma-2/",
      "html": "<p>The females are silvery all their lives and have no markings in their fins.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/callochromis-pleurospilus-kigoma-2/",
      "html": "<p>Callochromis pleurospilus are mouth breeders in the female sex.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/callochromis-pleurospilus-kigoma-2/",
      "html": "<p>The animals described here are descendants of fish that were collected at Kigoma Bay; fully coloured males of this local variant are also called “rainbow callochromis” because they are so colourful. An invalid trade name for this form is “Callochromis greshakei”.</p>"
    }
  ],
